**user**:
> I know I can turn a continuous vector in R into a number of discrete bins by calling cut and then table (not sure that that's the best way), but if any of the values aren't represented, I don't know how to then get out an ordered set of values. If I use levels from the cut, it will miss those missing bins, right?
**assistant**:
> `cut()` returns a **factor whose levels include all defined bins**, even bins with zero observations. So `levels()` will not omit empty bins—unless you subsequently call `droplevels()` or otherwise coerce/rebuild the factor.
> 
> ```r
> x <- c(1, 2, 8, 9)
> 
> breaks <- seq(0, 10, by = 2)
> bins <- cut(x, breaks = breaks, include.lowest = TRUE)
> 
> levels(bins)
> # "[0,2]" "(2,4]" "(4,6]" "(6,8]" "(8,10]"
> 
> table(bins)
> # bins
> #  [0,2]  (2,4]  (4,6]  (6,8] (8,10]
> #      2      0      0      1      1
> ```
> 
> The key is to specify the breaks yourself, rather than e.g. `breaks = 5`, if you need consistent bins across datasets:
> 
> ```r
> bins <- cut(x, breaks = c(0, 2, 4, 6, 8, 10),
>             include.lowest = TRUE)
> ```
> 
> For `dplyr`, preserve empty factor levels with:
> 
> ```r
> df |> dplyr::count(bins, .drop = FALSE)
> ```
> 
> `table()` likewise retains unused factor levels by default.
